[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6838b8cf-fec7-51b2-8373-943cee569878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365276} {:answer/id #uuid "8205c3ac-78de-533c-ae96-ff65f5828af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365277} {:answer/id #uuid "c11907f7-3548-559e-a061-4bcaf9c1b34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365278} {:answer/id #uuid "a7869954-b653-5a50-9765-f8085d861bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365279} {:answer/id #uuid "2bec3e13-4258-5707-beaf-f4fdea05190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bec3e13-4258-5707-beaf-f4fdea05190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3028a6b-dad4-502a-85ce-856d7efdc2e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "346646b9-04ff-58cb-a1cc-6c9b42073112" :question/math-academy-id "q-163551" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,m$ and the particle has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\pi \\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b396b80995e34fe348b3d059737b1eec3d4ce5a9d2e9138a8e7d4ed5c0945dc0.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=\\pi \\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ 32 & =8+k \\\\ k & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af5eca-1b90-5fe4-9c4b-927d2a442f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365352} {:answer/id #uuid "58cd2a77-5515-5752-ad84-4537d236a6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365353} {:answer/id #uuid "31af2651-f16b-59aa-957f-024743b9371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365354} {:answer/id #uuid "714cdf7d-69ae-5343-9e54-3467a962889f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365355} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8579b3-2d8a-5067-90b8-819ebd380eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31af2651-f16b-59aa-957f-024743b9371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365354} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a20e2d-0bf7-5a2c-9004-99c10ddffbbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7407b0b-36b6-5f0d-b3ce-2c90d7096c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-163573" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $(4x-2)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $1\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,m,$ find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cdb88c0c679ca9325df42c5c8c4feaea6ce6c0cf02655410158860d7b03d54dc.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(4x-2)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-(4x-2).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-(4x-2).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{2}+2x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,m/s$ when $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =-2{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ 2x(1-x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution of this equation for $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$ is $x=1\\,m.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfc85ec-dfe5-55f8-8344-1565d1cc6e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfc85ec-dfe5-55f8-8344-1565d1cc6e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365596} :answer-field/id #uuid "d38da886-33fc-549f-aacd-376765d1cdee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b80bca0b-0d46-5280-a67f-47b4201c0da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80bca0b-0d46-5280-a67f-47b4201c0da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365598} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc902c79-b3dd-53ba-8f15-9e0b419740ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88f28c66-d759-5b8d-b8f7-f31f33a24401" :question/math-academy-id "q-328436" :question/problem "The center of the hyperbola $9{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-54x=-56$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-54x & =-56 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-54x\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}-6x]\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9[(x-3)^{2}-9]\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}-81\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}^{2}}\\,-\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,3,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4a799a4-fcba-57a2-93a4-8f0c7b4e0152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4a799a4-fcba-57a2-93a4-8f0c7b4e0152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365642} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d6ac1d-7328-5250-958d-5f67f25922a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485d41b3-ae2a-558b-94dc-d65f80b383c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485d41b3-ae2a-558b-94dc-d65f80b383c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365644} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ad1fbe-7ae7-5ce2-a347-163e4a026338" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2a6942-d753-5843-937a-ae3bf9388a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333708" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4y{y}^{′}=6x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$ and ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right).$\n\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4y{y}^{′} & =6x \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6x+4y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{6x+4y{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈0$\nand\n${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a222e3-c0cd-51ae-8951-9d144bb362ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{3.4102\\times {10}^{17}}{r}+(1.464\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365719} {:answer/id #uuid "b42abe59-c840-5f25-8b3c-3cb37dd414c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{8.6204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365720} {:answer/id #uuid "b22fe792-9122-5033-97c9-a74562db32b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{17}}{{r}^{2}}+(1.464\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365721} {:answer/id #uuid "31d6b42f-5c6f-5ad6-9736-2da6165a7ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}-(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365722} {:answer/id #uuid "7571de97-cc39-59d7-88f0-5f746a4f0c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7571de97-cc39-59d7-88f0-5f746a4f0c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365723} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ced8e56-b5e1-5166-8485-98b96307c88d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccbff46-9435-57a4-ae53-244098c20951" :question/math-academy-id "q-336081" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(1.7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=11.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(7.4\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\\right)\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(1.7\\times {10}^{7}),$\n$g=11.8,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(7.4\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 11.8\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7})^{2}}{r}+(7.4\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 11.8\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23.6\\cdot (2.89\\times {10}^{14})}{r}+(54.76\\times {10}^{8})-(40.12\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{68.204\\times {10}^{14}}{r}+(54.76\\times {10}^{8})-(4.012\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+((54.76-4.012)\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(50.748\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4725da3c-09bc-5907-8640-bf7c25de282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+t}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365726} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb9fd6-1ec7-5ce0-b76b-cd19af257096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+t}⟨1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365727} {:answer/id #uuid "8edde89a-7624-55c8-a7af-35358692a3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365728} {:answer/id #uuid "15b9cb2b-f6cc-53ec-8fd9-5695161b485f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365729} {:answer/id #uuid "16ab3056-45e3-59ca-92b3-886c3dc6ed0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ab3056-45e3-59ca-92b3-886c3dc6ed0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365730} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ab9041-3f6f-5fe9-9218-b6f7268e04a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999eae56-9014-5e14-ac70-9c3dd9daada7" :question/math-academy-id "q-33627" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the unit tangent vector to the curve\n $r(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (0,\\infty )?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),\\,y(t),\\,z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+(1)^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Finally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d72036d-f499-5da5-84bb-887df07e964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.25\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(1.44\\times {10}^{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365749} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee8621-fb18-5939-921f-54b29640ca54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.25\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365750} {:answer/id #uuid "b02f327e-80bb-5bab-8017-7218b4e3cdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365751} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4a8447-1573-5ded-a399-9e542be9cd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{{r}^{2}}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365752} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cb986e-b3bd-5b2a-9628-5d2ddb111aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02f327e-80bb-5bab-8017-7218b4e3cdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365751} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ddeb15-24ce-5063-8338-21a9e8f7ab82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf83604-10d1-5208-b75e-d8b052cb3eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336343" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(5\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(1.2\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\\right)\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(5\\times {10}^{8}),$\n$g=9,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(1.2\\times {10}^{5}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 9\\cdot (5\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{r}+(1.2\\times {10}^{5})^{2}-2\\cdot 9\\cdot (5\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{450\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(1.44\\times {10}^{10})-(9\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(14.4\\times {10}^{9})-(9\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+((14.4-9)\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5c4875-9405-5f06-9640-986c7cb8c624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}-(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365756} {:answer/id #uuid "c952625b-a5ef-51b2-9fcc-8cf623dd17c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}+(3.6\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365757} {:answer/id #uuid "1e83eb17-5467-5a9e-a47d-b2c1302485dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365758} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d69858-66db-51a3-be7e-68960832e422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{0.8\\times {10}^{16}}{r}-(3.6\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365759} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff6cd7-0135-58fe-a92d-367df34e725a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{0.8\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e83eb17-5467-5a9e-a47d-b2c1302485dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365758} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61a3586-e160-5c0c-9ac7-bb9ff2f7f999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40b26c37-4285-5c4a-a408-42bee90acd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-336344" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(2\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=20\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(6\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\\right)\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(2\\times {10}^{7}),$\n$g=20,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(6\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 20\\cdot (2\\times {10}^{7})^{2}}{r}+(6\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 20\\cdot (2\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{160\\times {10}^{14}}{r}+(36\\times {10}^{8})-(80\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(3.6\\times {10}^{9})-(0.8\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+((3.6-0.8)\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdf13ef-7092-5823-b962-52c5b08a1f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf13ef-7092-5823-b962-52c5b08a1f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365763} :answer-field/id #uuid "f94d3759-a44a-5de9-adfc-871f4e6acb3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036f3fb2-564d-50d2-8910-431714bb2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036f3fb2-564d-50d2-8910-431714bb2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365765} :answer-field/id #uuid "7501eb5d-92fd-54ba-9cf5-acf3ae11bbe8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c8b28ec-b785-5775-9854-bc7fe8ba4126" :question/math-academy-id "q-336373" :question/problem "Consider a planet with radius $(4.2\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=5.6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object, \n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate.\n\n${v}_{e}≈($\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\\right)\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{r}\\right)+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r\\to \\infty ,$ the expression $\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}$ approaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\n{v}_{e}=\\sqrt{2gR}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values $R=(4.2\\times {10}^{8})$ and $g=5.6,$ we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{e} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{8})\\cdot 5.6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 4.2\\cdot 5.6)\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{47.04}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & ≈(6.86\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c44db92-d96d-57c9-a934-81a275972da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c44db92-d96d-57c9-a934-81a275972da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365768} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8fb2897-12de-5864-9088-ea47c31c14b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a71b979e-d62d-529b-a738-fdae3e60fda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a71b979e-d62d-529b-a738-fdae3e60fda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365770} :answer-field/id #uuid "9af8d35a-6232-51b9-81d8-b3162439889d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e4b410d-908b-51d6-be88-dbb25fbad4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336375" :